<h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

<h3>What is predictive analytics in simple terms?</h3>
<p>Predictive analytics uses your past data to estimate what is likely to happen next, such as next month's sales, which customers may stop buying, or which leads are most likely to convert. It gives you an informed estimate, not a guarantee.</p>

<h3>Do small businesses need a data scientist for predictive analytics?</h3>
<p>Not to begin with. Many useful predictions, such as sales forecasts, can be built with Excel forecasting tools or the forecasting features in a BI tool like Power BI. A data professional becomes useful when you want more accurate models or need to connect several data sources.</p>

<h3>How much data do I need to start?</h3>
<p>For time-based forecasts, at least one to two years of history helps, because it lets the model see seasonal patterns. For customer-level predictions such as churn, a few hundred customers with clear outcomes is a workable starting point, though more data usually improves accuracy.</p>

<h3>How accurate is predictive analytics?</h3>
<p>Accuracy depends on data quality, how stable your business is, and how well the question is defined. Predictions are estimates with a margin of error, so compare them with what actually happened each month and adjust the model over time.</p>

